WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.275
Lesson 2.5.

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.028
MEV and protecting your trades.

00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:06.882
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:06.882 --> 00:00:08.845
be able to set slippage and routing

00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:10.753
so your trades aren't easy targets.

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.343
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.343 --> 00:00:15.028
First, the key ideas.

00:00:15.028 --> 00:00:17.743
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:19.268
Then, your checklist.

00:00:19.268 --> 00:00:21.430
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:26.432
MEV (maximal extractable value) is profit taken

00:00:26.432 --> 00:00:29.971
by whoever orders transactions in a block.

00:00:29.971 --> 00:00:33.386
Some is harmless (arbitrage realigning prices).

00:00:33.386 --> 00:00:35.539
The kind that hurts you: (pushing the

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.692
price up), lets your trade execute at

00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:40.201
the worse price, then sells just after.

00:00:40.201 --> 00:00:43.136
Your slippage tolerance is its profit ceiling.

00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:46.278
Defences: First, Sandwich attack: a bot sees

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:49.371
your pending swap, buys just before you.

00:00:49.371 --> 00:00:54.449
Next, Tight but realistic slippage (e.g. 0.1–0.5% for

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:59.451
liquid majors, more only when you understand why).

00:00:59.451 --> 00:01:03.123
Then, MEV-protected / private transaction routing

00:01:03.123 --> 00:01:06.508
(many wallets and aggregators offer this).

00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:08.483
Also, Split large trades.

00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:10.038
Avoid thin pools.

00:01:10.038 --> 00:01:13.578
And finally, Don't broadcast huge swaps in illiquid pairs.

00:01:14.878 --> 00:01:19.773
Swapping $20,000: Too tight a tolerance (e.g. 0.05% on

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:24.579
a volatile pair) just makes the transaction fail, and

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:29.151
failed transactions still cost gas (Lesson 1.2).

00:01:29.151 --> 00:01:32.006
3%: up to ~$600.

00:01:32.006 --> 00:01:35.661
0.5%: up to ~$100.

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:39.237
Private / protected route: Transaction isn't visible in the

00:01:39.237 --> 00:01:42.396
public mempool, so it can't be sandwiched from there.

00:01:43.696 --> 00:01:45.156
Here's your checklist.

00:01:45.156 --> 00:01:46.691
Do it now, for real.

00:01:46.691 --> 00:01:48.994
First, Slippage set for this pair and

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:51.416
size, never left at a high default.

00:01:51.416 --> 00:01:54.551
Next, Protected routing on for larger swaps.

00:01:54.551 --> 00:01:56.478
And finally, Large orders split

00:01:56.478 --> 00:01:58.466
or routed through deep liquidity.

00:01:59.766 --> 00:02:00.976
Question 1.

00:02:00.976 --> 00:02:03.531
What limits how much a sandwich bot can take?

00:02:07.951 --> 00:02:10.996
The answer: Your slippage tolerance (and the pool's depth).

00:02:12.296 --> 00:02:13.541
Question 2.

00:02:13.541 --> 00:02:15.336
Why not set slippage near zero?

00:02:19.756 --> 00:02:22.452
The answer: Normal price movement makes the

00:02:22.452 --> 00:02:25.025
transaction revert, and you still pay gas.

00:02:26.326 --> 00:02:27.596
Question 3.

00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:29.831
Name two MEV defences.

00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:37.806
The answer: Any two: tight slippage, private/protected

00:02:37.806 --> 00:02:40.746
routing, splitting trades, using deep pools.

00:02:42.046 --> 00:02:43.291
Let's recap.

00:02:43.291 --> 00:02:45.361
Sandwich attack: a bot sees your

00:02:45.361 --> 00:02:47.856
pending swap, buys just before you.

00:02:47.856 --> 00:02:53.087
Tight but realistic slippage (e.g. 0.1–0.5% for liquid

00:02:53.087 --> 00:02:57.476
majors, more only when you understand why).

00:02:57.476 --> 00:03:01.194
MEV-protected / private transaction routing (many

00:03:01.194 --> 00:03:04.171
wallets and aggregators offer this).

00:03:04.171 --> 00:03:06.821
Split large trades: Avoid thin pools.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.636
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:10.636 --> 00:03:13.996
Next up, Lesson 2.6: Advanced execution:

00:03:13.996 --> 00:03:16.946
limit, TWAP and intent-based orders.
